heading: "California: changes in writing, signed before the work"
sub: What the Business and Professions Code says about written, signed change orders for home improvement and service and repair work.
---
<div class="wrap sec sec--tight rule">
  <article class="rule__body" aria-labelledby="h-plain">
    <p class="rule__verified">Verified on <time datetime="{{ california.verified }}">{{ california.verifiedText }}</time> against the official text on leginfo.legislature.ca.gov.</p>

    <h2 id="h-plain" class="h2 h2--sm">In plain words</h2>
    <ul class="rule__summary">
      <li>Section 7159 covers home improvement contracts where the total price is over $500. It says the contract and any changes to it are to be in writing and signed by both sides before the work they cover starts.</li>
      <li>It says a change order becomes part of the contract only if it is in writing and signed before the work it covers starts, and that a change order describes the scope of the change, the cost added or taken off, and the effect on the payment schedule.</li>
      <li>Section 7159.10 covers small service and repair contracts of $750 or less that meet its conditions. It says changes to those contracts are also to be in writing and signed or acknowledged.</li>
      <li>Both sections have more requirements than the parts quoted here. Read the full text at the links below.</li>
    </ul>

